  "instructions": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "primary_message": "Rinse the beef tendons under cold running water; then place into a bowl in a steamer insert along with the 1/4 cup soy sauce. Add a half cup or water to partially cover the tendons. Bring the water to boil; then reduce to a steady simmer and cook for 4 hours. (If you have a pressure cooker, you can cut down on the steaming time by half for a total of two hours cooking time instead of four.) Add additional water as needed to ensure that the bottom of the pot does not dry out.",
      "secondary_message": null
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "primary_message": "Remove the tendons from the pot and set aside the simmering liquid for another use. Let the tendons cool to room temperature; then refrigerate until the tendons are completely firm.",
      "secondary_message": null
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "primary_message": "Thinly slice the tendons into 1/8-inch slices. (The cooked tendons may be kept in the refrigerator for a week or more, so you may slice as much or as little you need each time.)",
      "secondary_message": null
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "primary_message": "Mix the tendons with the soy sauce, chile oil, vinegar, sugar, salt, and sesame oil. Ideally, let the dish rest for at least 2 hours at room temperature to allow the slices of tendon to fully absorb the flavors of the dressing. After 2 hours, taste a slice of the tendon and add more seasoning, as needed. Toss with the chopped cilantro and green onion. Serve as a cold dish.",
      "secondary_message": null
    }
  ],
